Z||000000||Cliff D.||05-07-2001||05:24 PM||||I haven't driven my fiero for about a year. It needed a new fuel pump (YUCK), and front exhaust manifold (DOUBLE YUCK), wich I finally fixed. Before I sell my reliable firebird (is that a contradiction?) and leave only my fiero for daily commuting, I need to ask a couple questions.<BR>Some fundamental info:<BR>The car is an '85 gt. I get a good "code 12" and no others. The check engine light blinks very nicely in closed loop mode, but has an occasional hiccup to either lean or rich. The idle surges independent of these glitches over a range of about 200 rpm. <BR>When I drained the oil which has sat in the pan during the down time, I had a bit of a gas odor in it and it seemed to be overfull. <BR>There are no obvious things wrong, but I would like some opinions on possible causes of this surging problem. I have a new 02 sensor on the car so it shouldn't be that. <BR>BTW--my factory service manual has this troubleshooting chart which says if there are no trouble codes and the ecm is happy, the next step is to "observe the injector spray while cranking"....Yeah right, that's gonna happen!<BR>Anyway, what I really need to know is what my potential fire hazard/oil leak expectations realistically are from dried gaskets, O-rings or whatever. Also, beside the plugs, dist. cap,pcv, belts, and filters (oil & fuel) is there anything that I should simply replace as preventive measure? (the egr valve and tube are fine, and the coolant level is good, and the coolant color is good except for a tiny, tiny trace of the "it's headgasket time" milkiness. Thanks guys/gals. ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Raydar||05-07-2001||05:31 PM||||Is the milkiness in the oil or the coolant? If it's in the oil, it might have come from condensation if the car has sat for over a year without being run. See if it returns after you change the oil. <P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 black Formula<BR>88 soon-to-be-V6 coupe||||reg||1
Z||000002||Cliff D.||05-07-2001||06:55 PM||||That's in the coolant recovery tank, and it's so insugnificant that I probably didn't need the mention it. I did want to add though that I have difficulty with cold starts and my oil seems to be slow to drain back to the pan on shut-down. I am sure my problem is dirt related, but I don't know where. Any recommendations on chemical treatment or heat cycling?||||reg||1
Z||000003||mrgone||05-07-2001||07:10 PM||||my friends dads garage had a 'teraclean'ing<BR>machine in it that cleans the engine without taking it apart<P>dont know anything more than that||||reg||1
Z||000004||Carrolles||05-09-2001||08:28 AM||||I used one of the off the shelf Engine Flush products I got from WalMart in my 85GT with pretty good results.  I believe it is basically kerosene or some other solvent.  <P>Pour it into the crankcase and let it idle for 20 minutes.  Then change the oil and filter.<P>Always read and follow the manufacturer's directions.<P>I also ran a 3 part de-carbonizer kit through it.  That helped a lot.<p>[This message has been edited by Carrolles (edited 05-09-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||rogergarrison||05-09-2001||04:50 PM||||I like putting a quart of trans fluid in crankcase a couple of days or so before a change once in a while. I wouldnt do a lot of freeway driving till you change it though. The solvents in trans fluid really clean out sludge and varnish, yet still provide lubrication. Engines ive done this too are always super clean inside whenever I go inside them.||||reg||1
